  "claim": "The leader of the Crimean Karaites followed Şahin Giray to the protectress, Catherine II, who was the country's longest-ruling female leader and arguably its most renowned.",
  "evidencia": "Page: Benjamin Aga. Sentence: Benjamin Aga (Karaim: Беньямин Агъа), who died in 1824, was the leader of the Crimean Karaites.\nPage: Catherine the Great. Sentence: Catherine II (Russian: Екатерина Алексеевна \"Yekaterina Alekseyevna\"; 2 May [O.S. 21 April] 1729 – 17 November [O.S. 6 November] 1796 ), also known as Catherine the Great (Екатери́на Вели́кая , \"Yekaterina Velikaya\"), born Princess Sophie of Anhalt-Zerbst, was Empress of Russia from 1762 until 1796, the country's longest-ruling female leader and arguably its most renowned, regardless of gender (although Peter the Great was the only Tsar officially designated as \"The Great\" ).",
  "evidence_text": "Page: Benjamin Aga. Sentence: Benjamin Aga (Karaim: Беньямин Агъа), who died in 1824, was the leader of the Crimean Karaites.\nPage: Catherine the Great. Sentence: Catherine II (Russian: Екатерина Алексеевна \"Yekaterina Alekseyevna\"; 2 May [O.S. 21 April] 1729 – 17 November [O.S. 6 November] 1796 ), also known as Catherine the Great (Екатери́на Вели́кая , \"Yekaterina Velikaya\"), born Princess Sophie of Anhalt-Zerbst, was Empress of Russia from 1762 until 1796, the country's longest-ruling female leader and arguably its most renowned, regardless of gender (although Peter the Great was the only Tsar officially designated as \"The Great\" ).",
